/// Terminal TUI on an alternate screen: the row layout of an agent-CLI —
/// a scrolling transcript on top, the input line pinned, a status row under
/// it. Immediate-mode rendering: the app re-draws the frame each turn with
/// `Screen`, the library handles the terminal control sequences.
///
///     import "koala/tui"
///
///     let screen = tui.Screen()
///     let layout = tui.Layout(screen)
///     let transcript = tui.View(layout.elastic())   // rows 1..H-2
///     let input_row  = layout.fixed_bottom(1)       // H-1
///     let status_row = layout.fixed_bottom(1)       // H
///
///     while true {
///         screen.frame_start()
///         for l in transcript.render() { screen.write(l) }
///         screen.write_at(status_row.row, 1, "[agent] model=auto")
///         screen.pin_input(input_row.row)
///         let line = screen.input("[agent] you> ") else { break }
///         transcript.append(line)
///     }
///     screen.leave()
///
/// On a terminal, output printed between turns scrolls within the region
/// above the input row (DECSTBM), so the input line and the status row stay
/// put while output streams in. On a pipe or a file every draw call is a
/// no-op — print your content yourself for piped runs, and the same program
/// runs either way. When the chrome changes (a terminal resize, a new row),
/// re-cut the layout and re-attach the view with `View.set_rect`; the
/// transcript, the scroll position and the follow mode all survive.
///
/// `Screen.input` is the global `koala/readline` editor: completion,
/// history and hooks all still apply, and the editor draws at whatever row
/// `pin_input` placed the cursor on. One editor means one input row per
/// screen.
///
/// For a multi-row input area (e.g. a boxed input), give `pin_input` the
/// area's position and the rows the output may scroll, and hand the same
/// position to `readline.set_input_area`:
///
///     screen.pin_input(20, 2, 2, 17)            // region 2..17, cursor (20, 2)
///     readline.set_input_area(20, 2, cols - 2, 3)
///     let line = screen.input("> ")
///
/// The editor then draws its rows inside the area (Ctrl-J starts a new row,
/// Enter submits). `View.at_tail` tells whether the window shows the tail;
/// `View.scroll_up` / `scroll_down` page it, and a `readline.KeyHook`
/// (see `readline.set_key_hook`) can page on Page Up / Page Down: the hook
/// draws, and the editor re-draws the area afterwards.
